this video is brought to you by swedish  pod 101 pod 101 courses are one of my  favorite ways to start learning a new  language online click the link in the  description for a free lifetime account  to give it a try  hello everyone welcome to the langfocus  channel and my name is paul in the  recent video on the swedish language we  focused on the history of swedish as  well as some of its major features but  swedish is also an incredibly expressive  language with colorful slang expressions  and idioms today we're going to focus on  some common slang and then we're going  to look at some widely used idioms  meaning bro or brother the main word for  brother is rur from which this is  obviously derived  what's happening  another common expression is  meaning what's up well it literally  means what is being done  this is like what's happening bro  i'm just chilling this comes from the  american english slang chill out or  chill  are you still hungover  is short for bark full which means  hungover yo  yo yo  yeah i saw you pass out on the sofa last  night  the formal meaning of  is literally deck both in the sense of a  floor and in the sense of decking  someone in other words hitting them but  in this slang usage it's an intransitive  verb like you decked on the sofa with  the meaning of you passed out this guy  should consider drinking a little bit  less  the formal word for beer in swedish is  a cognate of the english word ale  but there's also a slang word besh  it came from the phrase biased el  meaning bavarian beer which turned into  baes earl and then it was further  shortened to bash this may not be  classified as genuine slang since it's  been in use for a long time but it's at  least distinctly informal there's also  another informal word for beer  which originally came from italian  oh bro bad  in swedish  means good and oo is a negation prefix  like un in english so it's like saying  ungood in english which is not an  official english word but you could use  it in a slangy way and it would work the  swedish word is kind of like that the  formal way of saying bad is doalit or  inte bro which means not good  how's your beer  pretty bad a similar word is  cool uncool the formal way of saying it  is in the cool meaning not cool this  prefix can be added freely to lots of  different adjectives to create a new  slangy word langfocus cool pewdiepie  langfocus is uncool pewdiepie is cooler  you ain't swedish if you don't have  knicker bro that's just a fact another  word that can be used to mean cool is  fett which literally means fat in formal  swedish the  bill that's a cool car  another common slang word is this one  that means angry luck  humble  he got angry  i'm the black foreigner  he got angry because i said he was fat  but i meant it was cool  the rich guy got angry because someone  stole his champagne  this literally means griller as in  someone who grills steak but in slang it  refers to a snobby rich guy who likes to  flaunt his wealth in the bars of  stockholm these guys are famous for  panning as in panning for gold in slang  it means ordering two bottles of  champagne and dumping out the contents  of one of them just to show off how rich  they are  the good news is that people who do  stupid things like that with their money  usually don't stay rich for very long  in recent decades some slang has also  entered swedish from immigrant languages  in particular turkish but also arabic  and some others this slang is more  widely used in immigrant communities but  it's also used to a lesser extent by  young people in general  one such word is kef meaning bad the t  at the end here indicates the neuter  form  this comes from the arabic word kef or  kaif meaning pleasure or fun the meaning  became reversed from positive to  negative in swedish they kept that's bad  they are a gift  this means money and this is a loanword  from turkish  this means police this comes from  turkish inesis which means without  mirror an expression used in turkish to  refer to police in a negative way  meaning that they're shameless and can't  look at themselves in the mirror the  funny thing is that in swedish they just  say aina so they're referring to police  as mirrors rather than as people who  can't look in the mirror swedish  speakers don't only use slang they also  use lots of colorful idioms some of  which seem truly bizarre in translation  its literal translation is no danger on  the roof  it means no worries or there's nothing  to worry about  another idiom with a similar meaning is  some which literally means no cow on the  ice this is taken from a longer idiom  which literally means there is no cow on  the ice as long as the butt is still on  land  literally slide in on a shrimp sandwich  you say this when you were lucky and  something turned out to not require much  effort or work  on the other hand when you gave  something your best shot but you were  unsuccessful and it didn't turn out as  you expected you might say neuro cooked  let's cooktop stick literally now the  boiled pork is fried  some swedish idioms are similar to ones  used in english so mambeda from maliga  this literally means the way you make  your bed you have to lie in it in  english we have a similar idiom you've  made your bed now lie in it and there  are numerous variations of this idiom in  english basically it means you'll have  to live with the consequences of your  own actions  this means butterflies in the stomach  meaning something like an anxious  feeling  literally that's easy as a pancake and  it simply means that's really easy in  english we have a number of expressions  like this one of them is easy as pie  though that's a pretty outdated  expression and no one really uses it  anymore maybe that's something my  grandmother would say but another  similar expression might be it's a  cakewalk both expressions mean that  something's really easy to do  buy the pig in the bag this is similar  to the english idiom by a pig in a poke  poke meaning sac or bag in both  languages it means to buy something  without examining the item first so that  it might not be exactly what you expect  it to be it might be fake low quality or  broken etc  i'm sure i'm just barely scratching the  surface of swedish slang and idioms but  the great thing about social media is  that you can participate in the comments  so swedish speakers why don't you write  your favorite slang and idioms in the  comments down below and speakers of  other languages if you have any similar  slang or idioms in your languages you  can tell us about those too  if you're excited to learn more swedish  then click the link in the description  to visit swedish pod 101 or visit  langfocus.com  swedishpod i really recommend you check  out innovative languages courses whether  for swedish or one of the other 33  languages they offer click the link in  the description to get a free lifetime  account i also want to give a big shout  out to all the langfocus patreon  supporters especially the top tier  patrons whose names are on the screen  check out patreon.com langfocus and  check out the benefits of the various  tiers and everyone thank you for  watching and have a nice day  [Music]  you
 

